WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:23.560
How would you like to be better than you are? Suppose I said that with just a few changes

00:23.560 --> 00:30.680
in your genes you could get a better memory, more precise, more accurate, and quicker.

00:30.680 --> 00:36.680
Or maybe you'd like to be more fit, stronger, with more stamina. Would you like to be more

00:36.680 --> 00:43.980
attractive and self-confident? How about living longer, with good health? Or perhaps you're

00:43.980 --> 00:52.120
one of those who's always yearned for more creativity. Which one would you like the most?

00:52.120 --> 00:59.640
Which would you like if you could have just one? Creativity. How many people would choose

00:59.640 --> 01:03.840
creativity? Raise your hands, let me see. A few, probably about as many as there are

01:03.840 --> 01:12.120
creative people here, that's very good. How many would opt for memory? Quite a few more.

01:12.120 --> 01:19.540
How about fitness? A few less. What about longevity? Ah, the majority, that makes me

01:19.540 --> 01:25.180
feel very good as a doctor. If you could have any one of these, it would be a very

01:25.180 --> 01:33.420
different world. Is it just imaginary? Or is it perhaps possible? Evolution has been

01:33.420 --> 01:39.780
a perennial topic here at the TED conference. But I want to give you today one doctor's

01:39.780 --> 01:46.840
take on the subject. The great 20th century geneticist, T.G. Dobzhansky, who was also

01:46.840 --> 01:53.120
a communicant in the Russian Orthodox Church, once wrote an essay that he titled, Nothing

01:53.120 --> 02:00.280
in Biology Makes Sense Except in the Light of Evolution. Now, if you are one of those

02:00.280 --> 02:05.520
who does not accept the evidence for biological evolution, this would be a very good time

02:05.520 --> 02:09.440
to turn off your hearing aid, take out your personal communications device, I give you

02:09.440 --> 02:14.480
permission, and perhaps take another look at Catherine Schultz's book on being wrong,

02:14.480 --> 02:20.840
because nothing in the rest of this talk is going to make any sense whatsoever to you.

02:20.840 --> 02:29.960
But if you do accept biological evolution, consider this. Is it just about the past?

02:29.960 --> 02:37.420
Or is it about the future? Does it apply to others? Or does it apply to us? This is another

02:37.420 --> 02:44.580
look at the tree of life. In this picture, I've put a bush with a center branching out

02:44.580 --> 02:50.660
in all directions, because if you look at the edges of the tree of life, every existing

02:50.660 --> 02:56.780
species at the tips of those branches has succeeded in evolutionary terms. It has survived.

02:56.780 --> 03:05.020
It has demonstrated a fitness to its environment. The human part of this branch, way out on

03:05.020 --> 03:12.460
one end, is of course the one that we are most interested in. We branch off of a common

03:12.460 --> 03:19.420
ancestor to modern chimpanzees about six or eight million years ago. In the interval,

03:19.420 --> 03:27.660
there have been perhaps 20 or 25 different species of hominids. Some have come and gone.

03:27.660 --> 03:34.220
We have been here for about 130,000 years. It may seem like we're quite remote from other

03:34.220 --> 03:41.820
parts of this tree of life, but actually for the most part, the basic machinery of

03:41.820 --> 03:48.340
our cells is pretty much the same. Do you realize that we can take advantage and commandeer

03:48.340 --> 03:54.860
the machinery of a common bacterium to produce the protein of human insulin used to treat

03:54.860 --> 04:00.860
diabetics? This is not like human insulin. This is the same protein that is chemically

04:00.860 --> 04:08.300
indistinguishable from what comes out of your pancreas. And speaking of bacteria, do

04:08.300 --> 04:14.900
you realize that each of us carries in our gut more bacteria than there are cells in

04:14.900 --> 04:19.740
the rest of our body? Maybe 10 times more. I mean, think of it. When Anthony Damasio

04:19.740 --> 04:28.980
asks about your self-image, do you think about the bacteria? Our gut is a wonderfully hospitable

04:28.980 --> 04:35.020
environment for those bacteria. It's warm. It's dark. It's moist. It's very cozy. And

04:35.020 --> 04:38.340
you're going to provide all the nutrition that they could possibly want with no effort

04:38.340 --> 04:44.740
on their part. It's really like an easy street for bacteria with the occasional interruption

04:44.740 --> 04:52.220
of the unintended forced rush to the exit. But otherwise, you are a wonderful environment

04:52.220 --> 04:57.860
for those bacteria, just as they are essential to your life. They help in the digestion of

04:57.860 --> 05:04.500
essential nutrients, and they protect you against certain diseases. But what will come

05:04.500 --> 05:11.820
in the future? Are we at some kind of evolutionary equipoise as a species, or are we destined

05:11.820 --> 05:19.580
to become something different, something perhaps even better adapted to the environment? Now

05:19.580 --> 05:25.660
let's take a step back in time to the Big Bang. Fourteen billion years ago, the Earth,

05:25.660 --> 05:31.140
the solar system about four and a half billion years, the first signs of proto-life maybe

05:31.140 --> 05:37.020
three to four billion years ago on Earth, the first multi-celled organisms perhaps as

05:37.020 --> 05:46.380
much as 800 or a billion years ago, and then the human species finally emerging in the

05:46.380 --> 05:53.220
last 130,000 years. In this vast unfinished symphony of the universe, life on Earth is

05:53.220 --> 06:01.740
like a brief measure, the animal kingdom like a single, single measure, and human life

06:01.740 --> 06:08.980
a small grace note. That was us. That also constitutes the entertainment portion of this

06:08.980 --> 06:16.700
talk, so I hope you enjoyed it. Now, when I was a freshman in college, I took my first

06:16.700 --> 06:23.780
biology class. I was fascinated by the elegance and beauty of biology. I became enamored of

06:23.780 --> 06:29.780
the power of evolution, and I realized something very fundamental. In most of the existence

06:29.780 --> 06:35.700
of life in single-celled organisms, each cell simply divides, and all of the genetic energy

06:35.700 --> 06:41.980
of that cell is carried on in both daughter cells. But at the time multi-celled organisms

06:41.980 --> 06:49.820
come online, things start to change. Sexual reproduction enters the picture, and very

06:49.820 --> 06:55.780
importantly, with the introduction of sexual reproduction that passes on the genome, the

06:55.780 --> 07:04.540
rest of the body becomes expendable. In fact, you could say that the inevitability of the

07:04.540 --> 07:11.180
death of our bodies enters in evolutionary time at the same moment as sexual reproduction.

07:11.180 --> 07:17.660
Now, I have to confess, when I was a college undergraduate, I thought, okay, sex, death,

07:17.660 --> 07:24.740
sex, death, death for sex seemed pretty reasonable at the time, but with each passing year, I've

07:24.740 --> 07:30.140
come to have increasing doubts. I've come to understand the sentiments of George Burns,

07:30.140 --> 07:34.540
who was performing still in Las Vegas well into his 90s, and one night, there's a knock

07:34.540 --> 07:40.120
at his hotel room door. He answers the door. Standing before him is a gorgeous, scantily

07:40.120 --> 07:46.780
clad showgirl. She looks at him and says, I'm here for super sex. That's fine, says

07:46.780 --> 07:58.660
George. I'll take the soup. I came to realize, as a physician, that I was working toward

07:58.660 --> 08:05.240
a goal which was different from the goal of evolution, not necessarily contradictory,

08:05.240 --> 08:10.380
just different. I was trying to preserve the body. I wanted to keep us healthy. I wanted

08:10.380 --> 08:16.720
to restore health from disease. I wanted us to live long and healthy lives. Evolution

08:16.720 --> 08:23.660
is all about passing on the genome to the next generation, adapting and surviving through

08:23.660 --> 08:30.060
generation after generation. From an evolutionary point of view, you and I are like the booster

08:30.060 --> 08:34.980
rockets designed to send the genetic payload into the next level of orbit and then drop

08:34.980 --> 08:41.100
off into the sea. I think we would all understand the sentiment that Woody Allen expressed when

08:41.100 --> 08:46.020
he said, I don't want to achieve immortality through my work. I want to achieve it through

08:46.020 --> 08:55.640
not dying. Evolution does not necessarily favor the longest lived. It doesn't necessarily

08:55.640 --> 09:01.060
favor the biggest or the strongest or the fastest and not even the smartest. Evolution

09:01.060 --> 09:10.900
favors those creatures best adapted to their environment. That is the sole test of survival

09:10.900 --> 09:17.260
and success. At the bottom of the ocean, bacteria that are thermophilic and can survive

09:17.260 --> 09:22.540
at the steam vent heat that would otherwise produce if fish were there, sous vide cooked

09:22.540 --> 09:31.940
fish, nevertheless have managed to make that a hospitable environment for them. So what

09:31.940 --> 09:38.640
does this mean as we look back at what has happened in evolution and as we think about

09:38.640 --> 09:46.400
the place again of humans in evolution and particularly as we look ahead to the next

09:46.400 --> 09:56.400
phase? I would say that there are a number of possibilities. The first is that we will

09:56.400 --> 10:04.800
not evolve. We have reached a kind of equipoise. And the reasoning behind that would be first,

10:04.800 --> 10:09.920
we have through medicine managed to preserve a lot of genes that would otherwise be selected

10:09.920 --> 10:16.560
out and be removed from the population. And secondly, we as a species have so configured

10:16.560 --> 10:23.720
our environment that we have managed to make it adapt to us as well as we adapt to it.

10:23.720 --> 10:29.200
And by the way, we immigrate and circulate and intermix so much that you can't any longer

10:29.200 --> 10:37.240
have the isolation that is necessary for evolution to take place. A second possibility

10:37.240 --> 10:45.240
is that there will be evolution of the traditional kind, natural, imposed by the forces of nature.

10:45.240 --> 10:52.720
And the argument here would be that the wheels of evolution grind slowly but they are inexorable.

10:52.720 --> 10:57.720
And as far as isolation goes, when we as a species do colonize distant planets, there

10:57.760 --> 11:03.640
will be the isolation and the environmental changes that could produce evolution in the

11:03.640 --> 11:11.400
natural way. But there's a third possibility, an enticing, intriguing, and frightening possibility.

11:11.400 --> 11:18.720
I call it neo-evolution, the new evolution that is not simply natural but guided and

11:18.720 --> 11:26.840
chosen by us as individuals in the choices that we will make. Now, how could this come

11:26.960 --> 11:35.480
about? How could it be possible that we would do this? Consider first the reality that people

11:35.480 --> 11:41.240
today in some cultures are making choices about their offspring. They're in some cultures

11:41.240 --> 11:46.640
choosing to have more males than females. It's not necessarily good for the society

11:46.640 --> 11:54.640
but it's what the individual and the family are choosing. Think also if it were possible,

11:54.760 --> 12:01.760
ever, for you not simply to choose the sex of your child but for you in your body to

12:03.120 --> 12:09.760
make the genetic adjustments that would cure or prevent diseases. What if you could make

12:09.760 --> 12:16.200
the genetic changes to eliminate diabetes or Alzheimer's or reduce the risk of cancer

12:16.200 --> 12:23.200
or eliminate stroke? Wouldn't you want to make those changes in your genes? If we look

12:24.720 --> 12:33.520
ahead, these kind of changes are going to be increasingly possible. The Human Genome

12:33.520 --> 12:44.840
Project started in 1990 and it took 13 years. It cost $2.7 billion. The year after it was

12:44.840 --> 12:53.200
finished in 2004, you could do the same job for $20 million in three to four months. Today,

12:53.200 --> 12:57.280
you can have a complete sequence of the three billion base pairs in the human genome at

12:57.280 --> 13:03.820
a cost of about $20,000 and in the space of about a week. It won't be very long before

13:03.820 --> 13:09.660
the reality will be the $1,000 human genome and it will be increasingly available for

13:09.660 --> 13:16.660
everyone. Just a week ago, the National Academy of Engineering awarded its Draper Prize to

13:17.660 --> 13:23.660
Francis Arnold and Willem Stemmer II, scientists who independently developed techniques to

13:23.660 --> 13:29.660
encourage the natural process of evolution to work faster and to lead to desirable proteins

13:29.660 --> 13:36.660
in a more efficient way, what Francis Arnold calls directed evolution. A couple of years

13:36.660 --> 13:43.660
ago, the Lasker Prize was awarded to the scientist Shinya Yamanaka for his research in which

13:43.660 --> 13:50.660
he took an adult skin cell, a fibroblast, and by manipulating just four genes, he induced

13:52.000 --> 13:59.000
that cell to revert to a pluripotential stem cell, a cell potentially capable of becoming

14:00.180 --> 14:07.180
any cell in your body. These changes are coming. The same technology that has produced the

14:07.380 --> 14:13.440
human insulin and bacteria can make viruses that will not only protect you against themselves

14:13.440 --> 14:18.580
but induce immunity against other viruses. Believe it or not, there's an experimental

14:18.580 --> 14:25.580
trial going on with vaccine against influenza that has been grown in the cells of a tobacco

14:25.900 --> 14:32.900
plant. Can you imagine something good coming out of tobacco? These are all reality today

14:33.500 --> 14:40.500
and the future will be ever more possible. Imagine then just two other little changes.

14:41.480 --> 14:46.360
You can change the cells in your body, but what if you could change the cells in your

14:46.360 --> 14:52.120
offspring? What if you could change the sperm and the ova or change the newly fertilized

14:52.120 --> 14:58.480
egg and give your offspring a better chance at a healthier life? Eliminate the diabetes,

14:58.480 --> 15:03.760
eliminate the hemophilia, reduce the risk of cancer. Who doesn't want healthier children?

15:03.780 --> 15:10.780
And then that same analytic technology, that same engine of science that can produce the

15:10.900 --> 15:17.900
changes to prevent disease will also enable us to adopt super attributes, hyper capacities,

15:22.020 --> 15:26.900
that better memory. Why not have the quick wit of a Ken Jennings, especially if you can

15:26.900 --> 15:32.560
augment it with the next generation of the Watson machine? Why not have the quick twitch

15:32.580 --> 15:39.580
muscle that will enable you to run faster and longer? Why not live longer? These will

15:41.020 --> 15:47.680
be irresistible and when we are at a position where we can pass it on to the next generation

15:47.680 --> 15:54.680
and we can adopt the attributes we want, we will have converted old style evolution into

15:56.980 --> 16:01.780
neo evolution. We'll take a process that normally might require a hundred thousand years and

16:01.800 --> 16:08.720
we can compress it down to a thousand years and maybe even in the next 100 years. These

16:08.720 --> 16:15.720
are choices that your grandchildren or their grandchildren are going to have before them.

16:16.920 --> 16:23.920
Will we use these choices to make a society that is better, that is more successful, that

16:24.600 --> 16:31.600
is kinder or will we selectively choose different attributes that we want for some of us and

16:31.700 --> 16:38.700
not for others of us? Will we make a society that is more boring and more uniform or more

16:39.680 --> 16:45.660
robust and more versatile? These are the kinds of questions that we will have to face and

16:45.660 --> 16:52.660
most profoundly of all, will we ever be able to develop the wisdom and to inherit the wisdom

16:53.400 --> 16:58.660
that we'll need to make these choices wisely? For better or worse and sooner than you may

16:58.660 --> 17:03.720
think, these choices will be up to us. Thank you.

